        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 767,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 706,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the error function, erf, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the area under a bell curve, so it turns a distance measured in\nstandard deviations into a probability, and it makes a smooth S-shaped step\nfor edges and transitions.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from -1 to 1 through 0, steepest at 0 and flattening out quickly:\nerf(1) = 0.843, erf(2) = 0.995, erf(3) = 0.99998. It is antisymmetric:\nerf(-x) = -erf(x). erf(x / 1.414) is the chance a normal value lies\nwithin x standard deviations of the mean.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erf\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; far out it is exactly 1 or -1 in 32-bit\nfloats.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-3 to 3 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erf of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -3 to 3,\ndrawn from -1.1 (bottom) to 1.1 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erfc is 1 minus this, kept accurate far out.\nt.special.erfinv undoes it. t.special.ndtr is the same curve rescaled to\nthe normal distribution's 0 to 1 probability.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the error function, erf, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the area under a bell curve, so it turns a distance measured in\nstandard deviations into a probability, and it makes a smooth S-shaped step\nfor edges and transitions.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from -1 to 1 through 0, steepest at 0 and flattening out quickly:\nerf(1) = 0.843, erf(2) = 0.995, erf(3) = 0.99998. It is antisymmetric:\nerf(-x) = -erf(x). erf(x / 1.414) is the chance a normal value lies\nwithin x standard deviations of the mean.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erf\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; far out it is exactly 1 or -1 in 32-bit\nfloats.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-3 to 3 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erf of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -3 to 3,\ndrawn from -1.1 (bottom) to 1.1 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erfc is 1 minus this, kept accurate far out.\nt.special.erfinv undoes it. t.special.ndtr is the same curve rescaled to\nthe normal distribution's 0 to 1 probability."
        },
